Index the entries of a JAR file by a hash of their names.

A lookup of a JAR entry scanned the central directory header by header,
reading each 46 byte header and name through the 256 byte file buffer, and
kept the entries it had passed in a list of at most 256 byte arrays that was
searched linearly. Every class and resource of the application is looked up
in the system classes first and is not there, and a missing name was never
found in the list: it rescanned the directory from the first uncached entry
and entered the same entries in the list again. Loading a game spent about
five percent of its instructions in this code, and a run that reads many
resources up to fifteen percent.

Build an index of the central directory on the first search of a JAR: an int
array used as an open-addressing hash table with four bytes per slot, at most
two thirds of them in use, each holding the offset of an entry and eight bits
of the hash of its name. The directory is read in 512 byte pieces and no
object is allocated while it is read. A search hashes the name and reads the
header and name only of entries whose hash bits match, so a missing name
costs no file access. The first entry with a name still wins if several have
it. Names ending in a slash, the directories of the JAR, are indexed like
the other names: an explicit directory entry is found like a file, whatever
was searched before, and opens as an empty stream. Names longer than 256
bytes are left out, as in the old list. A JAR with more than
MaxJarCacheEntryCount (now 4096) entries, a directory too big for the slot
offsets or a failed allocation leaves the parser without an index, and its
directory is scanned for each search as when the entry cache is off. The
index takes about 4 KB for the 588 entries of the system classes, where the
list took about 25 KB for its first 256.

